@meta
id: 26-01
title: Send the file through a shell command
chapter: 26
type: lesson
skills: filter-bang
par: 15
minutes: 4
vscode: an extension for sorting, and nothing at all for the rest
open: routing-keys.txt

@brief
Every routing key the order service subscribes to lives in this file, and
it has been appended to for a year. Consumers do not care about the order,
but people reading it do. Sort it.

You could do this with `:sort`, which chapter 24 gave you. Do it the other
way instead, because the other way is the door to everything the shell can
do and vim cannot.

A filter hands a stretch of your buffer to a command on standard input,
waits, and replaces those lines with whatever the command wrote to
standard output. That is the whole mechanism. The command never sees the
file on disk, only the lines you gave it, and the lines you gave it are
gone the moment it answers.

@keys
| `:%!cmd` | send the whole file through `cmd`, replace it with the output |
| `:{range}!cmd` | the same, for a range of lines |
| `u` | undo the filter — one step, however many lines changed |

@hints
`%` is the range meaning the whole file, the same `%` you have been using
with `:s` and `:g`. What comes after the range is a `!`.
--
`!` on the ex command line means "run this in the shell". With a range in
front of it, the range goes in and the output comes back.
--
`:%!sort` and Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    :%!sort<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twelve keystrokes, and eleven of them would be the same for any command
you can name.

Say the shape out loud once, because the rest of the chapter is variations
on it: **range, bang, command**. `:%!sort`. `:1,20!sort`. `:'<,'>!column -t`.
The range is which lines get fed in. The command is anything on your
PATH. The output takes their place.

Two consequences, both worth internalising now.

The first is that the replacement is total. The command's stdout becomes
those lines, exactly, whatever it is. Nothing is merged, nothing is kept.
If `sort` printed one line, you would have one line.

The second is that this costs you nothing to try. A filter is a single
undoable change, so `u` puts every line back the way it was, and you can
run another. Filtering is not a careful operation. Try it, look at the
result, undo if it was not what you meant.

`:sort` would have been shorter here. It is also the only one of these
vim has. There is no `:jq`, no `:column`, no `:awk`, no in-house
`:base64 -d`, and there never will be — but they are all already
installed on the machine, and `!` is how you reach them.

@start routing-keys.txt
order.shipped
payment.captured
customer.created
order.cancelled
payment.refunded
customer.merged
order.placed
payment.failed
refund.requested
order.refunded

@expected routing-keys.txt
customer.created
customer.merged
order.cancelled
order.placed
order.refunded
order.shipped
payment.captured
payment.failed
payment.refunded
refund.requested
